.TH TABS 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tabs \- set terminal tab stops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tabs
.RI [ \-n ]
.br
.B tabs
.IR n1 [, n2 ,...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tabs
writes ANSI escape sequences to standard output that clear all tab stops
on the terminal and then set new ones.
It emits a carriage return and
.B ESC [ 3 g
(clear all tab stops), moves the cursor right to each requested column
and sets a stop there with
.B ESC H
(HTS), and finishes with a carriage return.
No terminal database is consulted; the terminal must understand these
sequences.
.PP
Columns are numbered from 1.
Without a column list, stops are set every 8 columns (at 9, 17, 25, ...).
Stops are set only up to column 1024.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \- n
Set a stop every
.I n
columns, at columns 1+\fIn\fR, 1+2\fIn\fR, and so on.
.I n
must be at least 1.
.TP
.IR n1 , n2 ,...
Set stops at exactly the listed columns.
Columns are separated by commas or spaces and must be strictly
increasing.
Several list arguments may be given; they are concatenated.
A list overrides any
.BI \- n
option.
.TP
.B \-\-
Ends option processing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An argument was not recognised, the increment was less than 1, the
column list was not increasing, or writing to standard output failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set stops every 4 columns:
.EX
$ tabs \-4
.EE
.PP
Set stops at specific columns:
.EX
$ tabs 10,20,40
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stty (1),
.BR tty (1)
.SH BUGS
The POSIX
.B \-T
.I type
option, the predefined formats such as
.BR \-a " and " \-c ,
and
.BI + n
increments in a column list are not supported.
List entries of 0 or greater than 1024 are silently ignored, and any
arguments after
.B \-\-
are ignored.
